WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.440
Today we are going to concoct some absolutely&nbsp;
insane fluid and smoke simulations. A common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.440
property of these simulation programs is that&nbsp;
they subdivide the simulation domain into a grid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.960
and they compute important quantities like&nbsp;
velocity and pressure in these gridpoints.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:29.520
Normally, a regular grid looks something&nbsp;
like this, but this crazy new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:36.960
throws away the idea of using this as a grid, and&nbsp;
uses this instead. This is called the Adaptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:43.120
Staggered-Tilted grid, an AST grid in&nbsp;
short. So what does that really mean?

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:50.640
The tilted part means that cells can be rotated&nbsp;
by 45 degrees like this. And interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.320
they typically appear only where needed, I’ll&nbsp;
show you in a moment. The adaptive part means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.800
that the size of the grid cells is&nbsp;
not fixed, and can be all over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.720
place. And even better, this concept can&nbsp;
be easily generalized to 3D grids as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.880
Now, when I first read this paper, two things&nbsp;
came to my mind, one, that is an insane idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:22.480
I kinda like it, and two, it cannot possibly&nbsp;
work! It turns out, only one of these is true.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:30.320
And I was also wondering, why? Why do all this?&nbsp;
And the answer is because this way, we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:37.440
better fluid and smoke simulations. Oh yeah! Let’s&nbsp;
demonstrate it through four beautiful experiments!

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.760
Experiment number one. Kármán vortex&nbsp;
streets. We noted that the tilted grid points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.680
appear only where they are needed, and these are&nbsp;
places where there is great deal of vorticity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.800
Let’s test that. This phenomenon showcases&nbsp;
repeated vortex patterns and the algorithm is hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:05.440
at work here. How do we know? Well, of course, we&nbsp;
don’t know that…yet! So let’s look under the hood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:14.240
together and see what is going on! Oh wow, look at&nbsp;
that! The algorithm knows where the vorticity is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.840
and as a result, these tilted cells are&nbsp;
flowing through the simulation beautifully.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.520
Experiment number two. Smoke plumes and&nbsp;
porous nets. This technique refines the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.160
grids with these tilted cells in the areas&nbsp;
where there is a great deal of turbulence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:43.840
and, wait a second. What is this? The net is&nbsp;
also covered with tilted cells. Why is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.200
The reason for this is that the tilted cells not&nbsp;
only cover turbulent regions, but other regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.520
of interest as well. In this case, it enables us&nbsp;
to capture this narrow flow around the obstacle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.920
Without this new AST grid, some of these&nbsp;
smoke plumes wouldn’t make it through the net.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:10.800
Experiment number three. The boat ride. Note that&nbsp;
the surface of the pool is completely covered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.880
the new tilted cells, making sure that the wake&nbsp;
of the boat is as detailed as it can possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:24.640
be. But in the meantime, the algorithm is not&nbsp;
wasteful, look, the volume itself is free of them.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.000
And now, hold on to your papers&nbsp;
for experiment number four.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:38.240
Thin water sheets. You can see the final&nbsp;
simulation here, and if we look under the hood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:47.760
my goodness, just look at how much work this&nbsp;
algorithm is doing. And what is even better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.120
it only does so where it is really needed it&nbsp;
doesn’t do any extra work in these regions.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:00.160
I am so far, very impressed with this technique.&nbsp;
We saw that it does a ton of work for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.160
and increases the detail in our simulations, and&nbsp;
helps things flow through where they should really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:13.680
flow through. Now, with that said, there is only&nbsp;
one question left. What does this cost us? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.400
much more expensive is this kind of AST grid&nbsp;
simulation than a regular grid? +100 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:27.680
computation time? +50 percent? How much is it&nbsp;
worth to you? Please stop the video and leave a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:35.840
comment with your guess. I’ll wait. Thank you! The&nbsp;
answer is none of those. It costs almost nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:43.120
and adds typically an additional 1% of computation&nbsp;
time. And in return for that almost nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.720
we get all of these beautiful fluid and&nbsp;
smoke simulations. What a time to be alive!

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.440
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

